Docker commands
1. Check whether Docker is working
docker --version
docker info
2. Image management
docker pull ubuntu
docker images
docker rmi IMAGE_ID
3. Container lifecycle
docker run -it ubuntu bash
You'll enter a new Linux shell inside the container.
Inside that container, try:
pwd
ls
exit
Now run these commands in your normal Linux terminal:
docker ps
docker ps -a
docker stop CONTAINER_ID
docker start CONTAINER_ID
docker rm CONTAINER_ID
4. Working with content inside containers
Use a running container for these commands.
docker exec -it CONTAINER_ID bash
This opens a shell inside an already-running container.
For copying files, first make sure the host file exists:
touch file.txt
Copy it into the container:
docker cp file.txt CONTAINER_ID:/tmp/
To copy a file back from the container:
docker cp CONTAINER_ID:/tmp/file.txt .
For the second command, /tmp/file.txt must already exist inside the container. You can create it using:
docker exec CONTAINER_ID bash -c "echo Hello > /tmp/file.txt"
5. Logs and inspection
docker logs CONTAINER_ID
docker inspect CONTAINER_ID
6. Volumes
docker volume create mydata
docker volume ls
docker volume inspect mydata
docker run -it -v mydata:/app/data ubuntu bash
Inside the container, run:
echo "Hello Docker" > /app/data/test.txt
exit
The volume stores data persistently, even after the container is removed.

