Experiment 11: JavaScript Program and Testing Using Karate
Aim
To create a simple calculator program in JavaScript and test its API using Karate.
Step 1: Create the Experiment Directory
mkdir Experiment
cd Experiment
Step 2: Create the JavaScript File
nano calculator.js
Code:
function add(a, b) {
    return a + b;
}

function subtract(a, b) {
    return a - b;
}

console.log("Addition:", add(10, 5));
console.log("Subtraction:", subtract(10, 5));
Step 3: Execute the JavaScript Program
node calculator.js
Output:
Addition: 15
Subtraction: 5
Step 4: Initialize Node.js Project
npm init -y
Step 5: Install Express
npm install express
Step 6: Create the API Server
nano server.js
Code:
const express = require("express");
const app = express();

app.get("/add", (req, res) => {
    let a = Number(req.query.a);
    let b = Number(req.query.b);

    res.json({ result: a + b });
});

app.get("/multiply", (req, res) => {
    let a = Number(req.query.a);
    let b = Number(req.query.b);

    res.json({ result: a * b });
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log("Server running on port 3000");
});
Step 7: Start the Server
node server.js
Step 8: Test the Addition API
Open another terminal and execute:
curl "http://localhost:3000/add?a=10&b=5"
Output:
{"result":15}
Step 9: Test the Multiplication API
curl "http://localhost:3000/multiply?a=10&b=5"
Output:
{"result":50}
Result
Successfully created a JavaScript calculator program and tested its APIs.

Step 10: Create Karate Feature File
Keep the Node.js server running in Terminal 1.

Open Terminal 2 and go to your Experiment directory:
cd Experiment


Create the Karate file:
    nano calculator.feature


Paste this code:

      Feature: Calculator API Testing
      
        Background:
          * url 'http://localhost:3000'
      
        Scenario: Test Addition
          Given path 'add'
          And param a = 10
          And param b = 5
          When method get
          Then status 200
          And match response == { result: 15 }
      
        Scenario: Test Multiplication
          Given path 'multiply'
          And param a = 10
          And param b = 5
          When method get
          Then status 200
          And match response == { result: 50 }



Save the file using Ctrl + O, Enter, then Ctrl + X.
Step 11: Run Karate Testing
In Terminal 2, execute:
        karate run calculator.feature


Karate will:
- Send requests to the calculator API.
- Check whether the HTTP status is 200.
- Verify addition returns 15.
- Verify multiplication returns 50.
Step 12: Expected Output







mervebozkir.com


Your terminal should display something similar to:
Feature: calculator.feature

scenarios: 2 | passed: 2 | failed: 0

features: 1 | passed: 1 | failed: 0













Experiment 12: Testing a Containerized Application Using Spring Boot
Aim
To develop and test a Spring Boot application using Docker and Kubernetes.
Step 1: Create Spring Boot Project
Create a Spring Boot project using Spring Initializr with:
•	Spring Web
•	Spring Boot Starter Test
Step 2: Create Controller
Create HelloController.java:
          package com.example.demo;
          
          import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
          
          @RestController
          public class HelloController {
          
              @GetMapping("/")
              public String hello() {
                  return "Hello SpringBoot - Experiment 12";
              }
          }
Step 3: Run the Application
      ./mvnw spring-boot:run
Open another terminal and test:
      curl http://localhost:8080/
Output:
Hello SpringBoot - Experiment 12
Step 4: Package the Application
      ./mvnw clean package
This creates an executable JAR inside the target directory.
Step 5: Create Dockerfile
Create a file named Dockerfile in the project root.
        FROM eclipse-temurin:21-jdk
        WORKDIR /app
        COPY target/*.jar app.jar
        EXPOSE 8080
        ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]
Step 6: Build Docker Image
        docker build -t experiment12 .
Step 7: Run Docker Container
        docker run -d -p 8080:8080 --name exp12 experiment12
Test the application:
curl http://localhost:8080/
Step 8: Check Kubernetes
kubectl get nodes
Step 9: Create Kubernetes Deployment
Create directory and file:
mkdir k8s
        nano k8s/deployment.yaml
deployment.yaml:
      apiVersion: apps/v1
      kind: Deployment
      metadata:
        name: experiment12
      spec:
        replicas: 1
        selector:
          matchLabels:
            app: experiment12
        template:
          metadata:
            labels:
              app: experiment12
          spec:
            containers:
              - name: experiment12
                image: experiment12
                imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
                ports:
                  - containerPort: 8080
Apply deployment:
      kubectl apply -f k8s/deployment.yaml
Step 10: Create Kubernetes Service
Create     k8s/service.yaml:
        apiVersion: v1
        kind: Service
        metadata:
          name: experiment12-service
        spec:
          type: NodePort
          selector:
            app: experiment12
          ports:
            - port: 8080
              targetPort: 8080
              nodePort: 30082
Apply service:
        kubectl apply -f k8s/service.yaml
Step 11: Test Kubernetes Application
        kubectl get pods
Forward the port:
        kubectl port-forward service/experiment12-service 8082:8080
Open another terminal:
        curl http://localhost:8082/
Output:
Hello SpringBoot - Experiment 12
Step 12: Test Cases
Test	Expected Result
Spring Boot application	Application runs successfully
Docker container	Application responds successfully
Kubernetes Pod	Pod is running
Kubernetes Service	Application responds successfully
Result
Successfully developed and tested a Spring Boot application using Docker and Kubernetes.






